insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, category, cover_url, content, read_time, seo_title, seo_description, published, published_at) values
($q$how-to-brief-a-video-editor$q$, $q$How to brief a video editor (and get a good first draft)$q$, $q$Most editing problems start before anyone opens the timeline. Here's what to send your editor on day one.$q$, $q$Video Editing$q$, '', $q$<p>Most editing problems start before anyone opens the timeline. A vague brief gets you a vague video, and then you spend three rounds of feedback saying what you could have said on day one.</p>
<p>Here's the short list we ask every client for. Fill it in once and the first draft usually lands much closer to what you pictured.</p>
<h2>Say what the video is for</h2>
<p>Is it an ad, a product demo, a YouTube episode, a Reel? The answer changes the first three seconds, the length, the music and even how fast we cut. "Make it look good" is hard to act on. "Get people to click the link at the end" is easy.</p>
<h2>Send the right material</h2>
<ul><li>The raw footage in a shared folder (Google Drive or Dropbox is fine)</li><li>Your logo, fonts and brand colors</li><li>Two or three videos you like, with a line on what you like about each</li><li>Where it will be posted and the shape you need: 16:9, vertical or square</li><li>Music you'd like to use, or a note on the mood</li><li>Your deadline, and the day you want to see the first draft</li></ul>
<blockquote>One reference video saves a page of explaining.</blockquote>
<h2>Pick one person to give feedback</h2>
<p>If three people comment separately, the notes often clash and the editor ends up guessing who's right. Choose one person to collect everything and send it in a single message. Time stamps help a lot: "at 0:42, cut the second sentence" beats "the middle feels slow".</p>
<h2>Plan for two rounds of changes</h2>
<p>A good first draft gets you about 80 percent of the way there. The second round fixes details, and a third is usually only needed when the goal changed halfway through. Knowing this up front keeps the schedule realistic.</p>
<p>If you'd like a hand with a project, <a href="/services.html#video-editing">see how our video editing works</a> or <a href="/contact.html">send us your brief</a>.</p>$q$, $q$5 min read$q$, $q$How to brief a video editor (and get a good first draft) | Tanvir.Studio$q$, $q$A short checklist for briefing a video editor, so the first draft lands close to what you imagined and you save a few rounds of changes.$q$, true, $q$2026-09-18T09:00:00Z$q$),
($q$motion-graphics-vs-animation$q$, $q$Motion graphics or animation? A quick way to choose$q$, $q$People use the two words as if they mean the same thing. They don't, and mixing them up gets expensive.$q$, $q$Motion Graphics$q$, '', $q$<p>People use these two words as if they mean the same thing. It gets expensive when you ask for one and wanted the other.</p>
<h2>Motion graphics: moving design</h2>
<p>Think animated logos, moving text, icons that slide into place, charts that build themselves. It's made from shapes and type, not characters, so it's quicker to produce and easy to keep on brand. It's the right pick for explainers, product videos, intros and social ads.</p>
<h2>Character animation: moving stories</h2>
<p>Here characters act, talk and show emotion. It takes far more time because every movement is drawn or posed by hand. It makes sense if you want a mascot that will be used for years, or if the story depends on feeling.</p>
<h2>A quick way to choose</h2>
<ul><li>You need to explain a product or process in under a minute: motion graphics.</li><li>You want a mascot your brand will use for years: character animation.</li><li>You need ten versions of one idea for social media: motion graphics with templates.</li><li>The budget is tight: start with a short motion piece and build from there.</li></ul>
<blockquote>Start with the smallest piece that does the job.</blockquote>
<h2>Build a system, not a one-off</h2>
<p>A five second logo animation and a set of lower thirds can make a whole channel feel finished. Once those exist, longer explainers and campaign videos get easier, because the style is already decided. Have a look at our <a href="/services.html#motion-graphics">motion graphics service</a> to see what's included.</p>$q$, $q$4 min read$q$, $q$Motion graphics or animation? A quick way to choose | Tanvir.Studio$q$, $q$Motion graphics and character animation are not the same thing. Here's the difference, and how to pick the right one for your next video.$q$, true, $q$2026-09-04T09:00:00Z$q$),
($q$brand-identity-basics$q$, $q$The five things every small business needs for its brand$q$, $q$A logo is only one piece. These five things keep your business looking the same everywhere.$q$, $q$Graphic Design$q$, '', $q$<p>A logo is only one piece. What makes a business look consistent is everything around it. If you're starting out on a small budget, these five things matter most.</p>
<h2>The list</h2>
<ul><li>A logo in a few versions: horizontal, stacked, and an icon-only one for small spaces</li><li>Two or three brand colors, with exact codes for screen and print</li><li>One or two fonts, and a note on where each is used</li><li>Templates for the posts you make most often</li><li>A short guide, two or three pages, showing how to use all of the above</li></ul>
<h2>Why bother</h2>
<p>People trust things that look the same everywhere. When your invoice, your Instagram page and your website feel like they come from one company, you look established, even if you started last month.</p>
<blockquote>Consistency is what turns a nice logo into a brand.</blockquote>
<h2>What to do first</h2>
<p>Start with the logo and colors. Then build templates for the one or two places where your customers find you. The guide can stay short. Our <a href="/services.html#graphic-design">graphic design service</a> covers all five, and you can add them one at a time.</p>$q$, $q$4 min read$q$, $q$The five things every small business needs for its brand | Tanvir.Studio$q$, $q$A logo is only one part of a brand. These five things keep a small business looking consistent, from the website to the invoice.$q$, true, $q$2026-08-21T09:00:00Z$q$),
($q$working-with-a-remote-creative-studio$q$, $q$Working with a remote studio: what to agree on first$q$, $q$Hiring a studio you'll never meet feels risky the first time. A few things on paper make it a lot less so.$q$, $q$Business$q$, '', $q$<p>Hiring a studio you'll never meet in person feels risky the first time. It usually isn't, as long as a few things are agreed on paper before work starts.</p>
<h2>Agree on the scope</h2>
<p>Write down what you're getting: how many videos or designs, which formats and sizes, and how many rounds of changes are included. Most disagreements come from two people picturing different things.</p>
<h2>Put the basics in writing</h2>
<ul><li>What will be delivered, and in which formats</li><li>Dates for the first draft and the final files</li><li>Price, deposit, and when the balance is due</li><li>An NDA, if your material is confidential</li><li>A line confirming that you own the final files</li></ul>
<blockquote>A clear scope matters more than where the team sits.</blockquote>
<h2>Keep feedback in one place</h2>
<p>Use a review link where you can comment on exact moments, instead of long email chains. It also leaves a record of every decision, which helps on the next project.</p>
<h2>Expect regular updates</h2>
<p>A short message at each milestone is enough. It keeps things moving, and small questions get answered before they turn into delays. If you're comparing studios, ask how updates work. The answer tells you a lot. You can also <a href="/contact.html">ask us</a> how we run projects.</p>$q$, $q$4 min read$q$, $q$Working with a remote studio: what to agree on first | Tanvir.Studio$q$, $q$Hiring a studio you'll never meet in person feels risky. These are the few things to agree on before work starts.$q$, true, $q$2026-08-07T09:00:00Z$q$)
